{#- ===========================================================
    Dietary marks — GF / V / LS, rendered next to an item name.

    Shared by the HTML surfaces that show the menu to a customer: /menu and
    the 1080p kitchen-facing board. Both call it through the same gate.

        {% from "partials/diet-marks.njk" import dietMarks %}
        {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed %}{{ dietMarks(item, menu.diets) }}{% endif %}

    THE GATE IS THE CALLER'S JOB and it is not optional on anything a
    customer reads. menu.json's `dietsConfirmed` is false until the kitchen
    has signed off on the classifications; a gluten-free badge is a claim
    somebody plans a meal around, and "we think so" is not a thing to say
    to a person with coeliac disease. The macro is left ungated so that the
    one surface that SHOULD show unconfirmed marks can: the /studio/
    tri-fold, which is the proof the client reads in order to confirm them,
    and which carries its own red not-kitchen-confirmed banner. That piece
    keeps its own copy of this markup — it is a fixed print artboard with
    its own class names and its own print rules, and folding it in here
    would couple a paper proof to a web page for no gain.

    `diets` is passed in rather than read from context so the macro has no
    hidden dependency on a global; the order of that registry is what makes
    GF/V/LS always read left to right the same way, everywhere.
    =========================================================== -#}
{%- macro dietMarks(item, diets) -%}
{%- set served = item.diet or [] -%}
{%- set onRequest = item.dietOnRequest or [] -%}
{%- if served.length or onRequest.length -%}
<span class="diet-marks">
    {%- for d in diets -%}
        {%- if d.code in served %}<abbr class="diet-mark" title="{{ d.name }}">{{ d.code }}</abbr>
        {%- elif d.code in onRequest %}<abbr class="diet-mark diet-mark--request" title="{{ d.name }} on request">{{ d.code }}*</abbr>
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
</span>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
